<GuideLayout
  title="Fabric Weight Selection Guide for Leggings, Bras and Gym Wear"
  type="FABRIC GUIDE"
  description="How to choose activewear fabric weight by GSM, opacity, compression, season, product category and target retail positioning."
>
  <p>Fabric weight is one of the fastest ways to change how activewear feels and performs. GSM affects coverage, compression, warmth, drape, cost and perceived quality. The right weight depends on product use, not a single universal number.</p>

  <h2>Typical GSM Ranges</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Lightweight tops: often selected for breathability and quick-dry comfort.</li>
    <li>Medium leggings: useful for yoga, studio training and daily athleisure.</li>
    <li>Heavy compression leggings: better for squat-proof coverage and shaping.</li>
    <li>Hoodies and sweatpants: require higher GSM for structure and premium hand feel.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common Buyer Mistakes</h2>
  <p>Choosing a heavier fabric does not automatically mean better quality. If the yarn, knit structure or spandex recovery is weak, the garment may still feel stiff, hot or unstable after washing.</p>

  <h2>How Fitclo Tests Fabric Weight</h2>
  <p>Fitclo reviews GSM together with stretch recovery, opacity, hand feel, colorfastness, shrinkage and garment fit. This gives brands a more reliable decision than selecting fabric by weight alone.</p>
</GuideLayout>
